9.text:
In a gas filled chamber the charge produced in unit time is

* $I/e$ ion pairs $s^{-1}$ 

and the power which has to be absorbed in the gas in order to produce them
is

$ \dot E = I\,W/e $

$W/e\simeq 33.97~{\rm V}$ for air.  Hence at the focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\,\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
       =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the rate at which energy is imparted to the air which is traversed by
the beam. The air which is traversed by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=h_{f}w_{f}=0.6\,{\rm cm}\times0.030\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and for a chamber of active length $L$,

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a} A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times 1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\times15.1\,{\rm cm}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
so that the air kerma rate in the beam, the air absorbed dose rate,
on the usual $K\approx D$ approximation for a few 10 keV photons, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\dot D_{a}= {\dot E\over m_{a}}
        ={I\,W/e\over m_{a}}
        ={2.0\times10^{-12}\times 33.97\over3.51\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in the focused $0.3\times6\,{\rm mm}$
beam.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
tissue kerma rate at the focus is essentially the same number, 
$\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{a}$.

For a horizontal raster the time a given surface element spends in the
beam is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= {w_{f}\over v_{s}}={w_{f}\over{\rm step/t_{exp}}}
     ={\rm ratio\; quoted\; above}
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the numbers quoted $t_{d}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}$ per
projection.  The dose delivered to one surface element in one energy
projection is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx 3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx 4~\mu {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per energy image, about $0.004\,{\rm mGy}$ per projection, the familiar
$ \dot D\approx I\,W/(e\,\rho_{a}A_{f}L)$ result.  With the usual
$W=33.97\,{\rm eV}$, $W/e=33.97\,{\rm V}$ this is

$D_{1}\simeq 3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 3.9\times10^{-4}{\rm mrad}$

per 20 ms dwell to the peak of the $0.3\,{\rm mm}$ focused beam, essentially
the air kerma at the focus, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ being assumed.  For a conventional 3–5 energy
multiple-energy scan this is $ \sim 1\!-\!2\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$,
i.e. $\sim 10\!-\!20\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ total surface exposure, $ <0.05\,{\rm mGy}$,
in good agreement with the very low dose quoted for synchrotron
phase-contrast micro-tomography.  The $~4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection is
the often quoted peak surface dose; the mean dose averaged over the
$w_{M}/w_{f}\approx 50/0.3\approx167$ raster points which cover the
$50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator field of view is $D_{\rm av}\approx
D_{1}w_{f}/w_{M}\sim2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}$ per projection,
i.e. $\sim 20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ mean surface dose per energy step over
the whole scanned field.  This is the $ \sim \mu{\rm Gy}$–level dose regime
quoted for the Laue monochromator system. ∎

3.text:
The air kerma which is measured in the chamber is

* $\dot Q=I/e$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$ 
* $\dot E=\dot Q\,W$  with $W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$ per ion pair in air

so that for the chamber which is on the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=\frac{I}{e}W e =I\,W
        =2.0\times10^{-12}\;{\rm A}\times33.97\;{\rm eV}
        =6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is actually illuminated.  With

$A_{b}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}$

and a depth of air in the beam $l=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=A_{b}l=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}\;,
\qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{b}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
       0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
\end{equation*}

the air kerma rate on the beam axis is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}=\frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
         =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}}
         \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
         \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{t}\approx\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{a}$
the same air kerma rate is the dose rate in soft tissue while the tissue
element is under the monochromatic $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot k_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\Phi_{0}\simeq\dot D_{t}/\big(E\,\mu_{en}/\rho\big)\approx
1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ on the axis.

A material point is in the $w_{0}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ beam for

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}= \frac{w_{0}}{v_{s}}
       =\frac{w_{0}}{\rm scan\;step/exposure\;time}
\end{equation*}

while the scanning table is moved.  In the notation used

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}= \frac{w_{0}}{\rm scan\;step/exposure\;time}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time of one tissue element under the beam for one energy
projection.  The dose delivered to the element in that projection is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,\tau_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic projection on the beam axis,
essentially the air kerma at the focus.  With the usual $W/e$ value of
$33.7\!-\!34.0\;{\rm eV}$ this is $3\!-\!5\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy.

In a conventional two– or three– energy Laue scan the dose adds
incoherently.  For the often quoted three energy set the cumulative
surface dose is

$D_{3}\approx3D_{1}\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\;{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\;
\mu{\rm Gy}$

and for a two energy set $D_{2}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ on the beam
axis.  The $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection, $\sim0.004\;{\rm mGy}$,
is the conventionally quoted single–energy surface dose; the quoted
$\sim 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ is the instantaneous dose rate under the
$33\;{\rm keV}$ monochromatic focus.  This is the dose which is normally
quoted as the synchrotron monochromatic imaging dose, the monochromator
$50\;{\rm mm}\!\times\!6\;{\rm mm}$ to $0.30\;{\rm mm}\!\times\!6\;{\rm mm}$
demagnification being already embodied in the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ focus
measurement.

11.text:
In an air filled chamber the air kerma rate which is for 33 keV the
absorbed dose rate in air is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{ {\dot Q}W/e}{m_{air}}  ,
\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{irr}  ,
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air.  In the focal spot the air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
V_{irr}=A_{f}\,L_{c}  ,
\qquad 
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-6}\;{\rm m^{2}},
\end{equation*}

so that with $L_{c}=0.151\;{\rm m}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{irr}=2.72\times10^{-7}\ {\rm m^{3}},\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{irr}=1.293\,{\rm kg\,m^{-3}}V_{irr}
      \simeq3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The power deposited in the irradiated air is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = {\dot Q}W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
       =6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W},
\end{equation*}

hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \dot E/m_{air}\simeq
\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.51\times10^{-7}}
\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  For 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}\approx
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{air},
\end{equation*}

so that the tissue absorbed dose rate in the beam is the same as the
air kerma rate quoted above.

A surface element which is carried through the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focal spot with a speed $v={\rm step}/t_{exp}$ stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
\tau =\frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the time normally quoted for a Laue scan.  The monochromatic surface
dose per passage, i.e. per energy step, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\Delta D_{1}\approx \dot K_{air}\,\tau
      \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $33.0\,{\rm keV}$ exposure, essentially the air kerma dose
$ \Delta D_{1}= {\dot Q}W\tau/e m_{air}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ at the
surface.  This is the dose which a given surface point receives in one
monochromatic frame.

If the whole Laue energy range which is illuminated on the crystal,
$W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$ in the dispersive direction, is sampled with the
$f_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot the number of independent
monochromatic steps which are normally acquired is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

the well known $50\,{\rm mm}/0.30\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification of the
bent Laue optics.  Summed over the complete multi-energy scan

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}\simeq N_{E}\,\Delta D_{1}
      \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy} ,
\end{equation*}

about $0.64\;{\rm mGy}$, $\approx0.064\;{\rm cGy}$, cumulative surface
absorbed dose for a full $\approx167$-energy Laue scan.  The
$\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ figure is the conventionally quoted dose per
monochromatic $33\,{\rm keV}$ frame; it is $ \approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$
integrated over the $\approx$50 mm/0.30 mm $\approx$167 energy steps of
a complete multiple-energy image.  Neglecting the $N_{E}$ factor this
is often quoted as $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step, i.e.
$\sim1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ air/tissue kerma rate at the
focus. ∎

6.text:
In the air the energy which is absorbed in the volume which is actually
illuminated is

* $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm\;C\,s^{-1}}$

* $W_{air}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV\,ion^{-1}}$

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{I}{e}W_{air}= \frac{I}{e}W_{air}
          = \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
            \times33.97\;{\rm eV\;s^{-1}}
          =4.24\times10^{8}\;{\rm eV\;s^{-1}}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the energy absorbed per second in the air which is in the beam.
For the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and for a chamber depth $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
          \times0.2718\,{\rm cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}
        =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{air}}=
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air and, to the usual $W/e$ approximation, in tissue
$\mu_{en}/\rho_{t}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the centre of the focus when the point is continuously in the
beam.  With the horizontal scanning the time a given surface element
spends in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beam is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
     \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}

the $w_{f}/\Delta x$ being the duty factor usually quoted for the scan.
Hence the dose actually delivered to a surface element in one
illumination, i.e. per energy point, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\;t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm \;Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per energy step at the point of illumination,
i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ air-kerma per scan step at the 33 keV
monochromatic point.  This is the conventional “dose per pixel’’ for the
Laue-scanner.

In integrated form over the whole horizontal aperture which is
effectively scanned, $w_{m}=50\;{\rm mm}$, the number of beam
widths which have to be visited is $w_{m}/w_{f}\simeq 50/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$.  The dose summed over the $w_{m}$ field which is
normally quoted as the cumulative surface dose of the multiple-energy
scan is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm cum}}\simeq D_{1}\frac{w_{m}}{w_{f}}
      \approx3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\times166.7\approx
      6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete horizontal scan at one nominal energy, viz. $\approx 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ air, $\approx 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ tissue, for the 50 mm
aperture.  The $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s illumination is the
dose actually received by any one surface element during one
energy step; multiplying by the number of energy steps used in the
multiple-energy protocol gives the total multiple-energy surface dose.
This is the usual $\sim$ mGy level quoted for synchrotron Laue
micro-imaging.

