A surface element which is carried through the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focal spot with a speed $v={\rm step}/t_{exp}$ stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
\tau =\frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the time normally quoted for a Laue scan.  The monochromatic surface
dose per passage, i.e. per energy step, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\Delta D_{1}\approx \dot K_{air}\,\tau
      \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $33.0\,{\rm keV}$ exposure, essentially the air kerma dose
$ \Delta D_{1}= {\dot Q}W\tau/e m_{air}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ at the
surface.  This is the dose which a given surface point receives in one
monochromatic frame.

If the whole Laue energy range which is illuminated on the crystal,
$W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$ in the dispersive direction, is sampled with the
$f_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot the number of independent
monochromatic steps which are normally acquired is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

the well known $50\,{\rm mm}/0.30\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification of the
bent Laue optics.  Summed over the complete multi-energy scan

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}\simeq N_{E}\,\Delta D_{1}
      \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy} ,
\end{equation*}

about $0.64\;{\rm mGy}$, $\approx0.064\;{\rm cGy}$, cumulative surface
absorbed dose for a full $\approx167$-energy Laue scan.  The
$\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ figure is the conventionally quoted dose per
monochromatic $33\,{\rm keV}$ frame; it is $ \approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$
integrated over the $\approx$50 mm/0.30 mm $\approx$167 energy steps of
a complete multiple-energy image.  Neglecting the $N_{E}$ factor this
is often quoted as $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step, i.e.
$\sim1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ air/tissue kerma rate at the
focus. ∎

public_text.6:
In the air the energy which is absorbed in the volume which is actually
illuminated is

* $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm\;C\,s^{-1}}$

* $W_{air}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV\,ion^{-1}}$

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{I}{e}W_{air}= \frac{I}{e}W_{air}
          = \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
            \times33.97\;{\rm eV\;s^{-1}}
          =4.24\times10^{8}\;{\rm eV\;s^{-1}}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the energy absorbed per second in the air which is in the beam.
For the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and for a chamber depth $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
          \times0.2718\,{\rm cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}
        =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{air}}=
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air and, to the usual $W/e$ approximation, in tissue
$\mu_{en}/\rho_{t}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the centre of the focus when the point is continuously in the
beam.  With the horizontal scanning the time a given surface element
spends in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beam is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
     \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}

the $w_{f}/\Delta x$ being the duty factor usually quoted for the scan.
Hence the dose actually delivered to a surface element in one
illumination, i.e. per energy point, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\;t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm \;Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per energy step at the point of illumination,
i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ air-kerma per scan step at the 33 keV
monochromatic point.  This is the conventional “dose per pixel’’ for the
Laue-scanner.

In integrated form over the whole horizontal aperture which is
effectively scanned, $w_{m}=50\;{\rm mm}$, the number of beam
widths which have to be visited is $w_{m}/w_{f}\simeq 50/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$.  The dose summed over the $w_{m}$ field which is
normally quoted as the cumulative surface dose of the multiple-energy
scan is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm cum}}\simeq D_{1}\frac{w_{m}}{w_{f}}
      \approx3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\times166.7\approx
      6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete horizontal scan at one nominal energy, viz. $\approx 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ air, $\approx 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ tissue, for the 50 mm
aperture.  The $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s illumination is the
dose actually received by any one surface element during one
energy step; multiplying by the number of energy steps used in the
multiple-energy protocol gives the total multiple-energy surface dose.
This is the usual $\sim$ mGy level quoted for synchrotron Laue
micro-imaging.

3.text:
The ionisation current is the energy which is put into the air which is
actually seen by the 0.30 mm $\times$ 6.0 mm focus.  With $W/e\simeq33.97\,{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =I\,W/e=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        =6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is deposited in the air that is in the beam.

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\ {\rm mm}\times6.0\ {\rm mm}=0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is irradiated.  Hence the air kerma rate in the
focused spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot E/m_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at the focus.  At 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\Bigr)_{t}\simeq\Bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\Bigr)_{a}=0.328\ {\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
so that to the usual kerma $\approx$ dose approximation

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is the instantaneous tissue kerma/absorbed dose rate while a surface
element is under the 0.30$\times$6 mm monochromatic focus.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\ {\rm mm}$ wide focus for the
quoted dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}
per monochromatic projection.  The dose actually received in one
monochromatic dwell is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\Delta D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
             \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
           \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per 33 keV, 20 ms illumination, $D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}$ with
the numbers quoted, i.e. $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in-beam.  This
is the conventionally quoted synchrotron micro-beam surface dose per
energy step, $\approx1.0\!-\!1.5\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a normal 2–3 energy Laue set.

The 50 mm horizontal illumination on the Laue crystal is demagnified to
the 0.30 mm focus.  The number of essentially independent monochromatic
steps which make up the 50 mm dispersive width is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50\ {\rm mm}}{0.30\ {\rm mm}}
       \approx1.67\times10^{2}\;,
\end{equation*}
the well known $50/0.30$ demagnification of the bent Laue optics.  Summed
over the whole Laue scan the integrated surface kerma normally quoted
as the cumulative multiple-energy dose is

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}\simeq N_{E}\,\Delta D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times
3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}
\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
for a complete $\approx167$ step Laue scan, i.e. $0.64\;{\rm cGy}$ of
surface air/tissue kerma integrated over the 50 mm field.  The
$\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell is the dose a single surface
element receives in one monochromatic frame; the $ \sim 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$
is the usual integrated multiple-energy value obtained by multiplying
the per-dwell $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ by $W_{i}/w_{f}\approx167$.  Neglecting
the explicit $N_{E}$ summation this is the $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy step, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in-beam, surface dose
quoted for the 33 keV Laue micro-imaging system.

6.text:
The air kerma which can be read off the chamber is the conventional
$W/e$ kerma

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot Q\,W/e}{m_{a}}\;,
\qquad \dot Q=I ,
\end{equation*}
$W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for dry air, in the air which is actually seen
by the beam at the focus.  With the $0.30{\rm \;mm}\times6.0{\rm \;mm}$
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}
     =1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
       2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

in the beam.  The power put into the irradiated air is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
      =6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that at the focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
         \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
         \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air.  For 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}\simeq
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{a}=0.328\ {\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
so that the same numerical rate is the kerma/ absorbed dose rate in
soft tissue while the tissue element is under the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm}$
monochromatic focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

A surface element which is translated through the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
focal spot stays in the beam for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}\approx 0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}
quoted for the scan.  The monochromatic surface dose received in one
pass of the focus, i.e. per energy frame at 33 keV, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\Delta D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,\tau_{d}
          \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
          \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV exposure, $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
with the nominal numbers.  With the usual $W/e=33.97{\rm V}$ this is the
conventionally quoted $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ air-kerma-equivalent surface
dose per 20 ms monochromatic dwell, $ \dot D\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
under the beam.  For a conventional $n_{E}=2\!-\!3$ energy Laue set this
is

\begin{equation*}
D_{n_{E}}\simeq n_{E}\Delta D_{1}\approx 8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
on the entrance surface, the $\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ level normally quoted
for synchrotron Laue micro-imaging.

If the whole dispersive width which is illuminated on the bent Laue
crystal is sampled, $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ in the dispersive direction,
the number of independent monochromatic steps that can be stepped through
with the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}
the $50/0.30$ demagnification of the Laue optics.  Summed over the
complete multi-energy scan the integrated surface air kerma, and with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the integrated tissue
absorbed dose, accumulated over the  $\approx 167$ energy steps is

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}\simeq N_{E}\,\Delta D_{1}
       \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

about $0.64\;{\rm mGy}\approx6.4\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}$ cumulative
surface dose for a full 50 mm/0.30 mm Laue scan.  The $ \approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic 20 ms dwell is the dose actually
received by a given surface element in one energy projection; the
$\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ value is the dose summed over the $\approx167$
independent energy steps which constitute a complete multiple-energy
Laue data set.  This is the $ \sim\!10^{-6}\,$Gy per frame, $\sim\!10^{-4}\,$Gy
integrated, dose regime normally quoted for the bent-crystal
synchrotron multiple-energy system. ∎

