surface dose for a full 50 mm/0.30 mm Laue scan.  The $ \approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic 20 ms dwell is the dose actually
received by a given surface element in one energy projection; the
$\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ value is the dose summed over the $\approx167$
independent energy steps which constitute a complete multiple-energy
Laue data set.  This is the $ \sim\!10^{-6}\,$Gy per frame, $\sim\!10^{-4}\,$Gy
integrated, dose regime normally quoted for the bent-crystal
synchrotron multiple-energy system. ∎

3.text:
The 2.0 pA reading is the ionisation produced in the air which is actually
seen by the 0.30 × 6.0 mm focal spot.  With $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for
dry air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power which has to be deposited in the air in the focus.  
For the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

in the beam, so that the air kerma rate in the monochromatic focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot E/m_{a}= I\,W/e/m_{a}
          \approx6.8\times10^{-11}/3.5\times10^{-7}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

33 keV on axis.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\ {\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ the same numerical rate is the tissue kerma/absorbed-dose
rate while a surface element is under the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm}$
monochromatic spot,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

A surface element is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide spot for the quoted
transit

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic stop.  The monochromatic entrance dose actually
received in one dwell, i.e. the conventionally quoted dose per energy
frame, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\tau_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per 33.0 keV, 20 ms illumination, $ \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in-beam.
For a normal $n_{E}=2\!-\!3$ energy Laue set this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{n_{E}}\simeq n_{E}D_{1}\sim8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}\sim1.0\!-\!1.5\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

on the entrance surface, the familiar $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy
synchrotron micro-beam figure.

The bent Laue optics demagnify $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ on the crystal to
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the sample.  The number of essentially
independent monochromatic positions which build up the 50 mm dispersive
field is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq W_{i}/w_{f}\approx 50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}.
\end{equation*}

Summed over the complete multi-energy Laue sweep the integrated surface
air-kerma $\simeq$ tissue absorbed dose normally quoted as the cumulative
dose of the whole 50 mm field is

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx6.4\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}\approx0.064\ {\rm cGy}$
air $\simeq$ tissue entrance kerma for a full $\approx 167$-step Laue
scan at 33 keV.  $D_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ is the dose a given
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm}$ surface element actually receives in one
$0.02\;{\rm s}$ monochromatic frame; $N_{E}D_{1}\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ is the
$W_{i}/w_{f}$ integrated cumulative surface dose built up over the 50 mm
dispersive aperture of the multiple energy acquisition.  Neglecting the
explicit $N_{E}$ summation this is the $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in-beam surface dose quoted for
the 33 keV Laue micro-imaging system. ∎

